1432=356.	How have you come here alone from @wSN{Aratta}?”} 

{357 = 357-387}
1461=357.	Holy @wDN{Lugalbanda} answered her: 
1465=358.	@q[Lugalbanda,Inana]{“What @wRN{Enmerkar} son of @wDN{Utu} quoth and what he says, 
1469=359.	what your brother quoth and what he says, 
1473=360.	is: 
1477=361.	@q[Enmerkar,Lugalbanda]{“Once upon a time my princely sister holy @wDN{Inana} summoned me in her holy heart from the mountains, 
1481=362.	had me enter brick-built @wSN{Kulaba}. 
1485=363.	Where there was a marsh then in @wSN{Unug}, 
1489=364.	it was full of water. 
1493=365.	Where there was any dry land, Euphrates poplars grew there. 
1497=366.	Where there were reed thickets, old reeds and young reeds grew there. 
1501=367.	Divine @wDN{Enki} who is king in @wSN{Eridu} tore up for me the old reeds, 
1505=368.	drained off the water completely. For fifty years I built, 
1509=369.	for fifty years I was successful. Then the @wEN{Martu} peoples, 
1513=370.	who know no agriculture, arose in all @wGN{Sumer} and @wGN{Akkad}. 
1517=371.	But the wall of @wSN{Unug} extended out across the desert like a bird net. 
1521=372.	Yet now, here in this place, 
1525=373.	my attractiveness to her has dwindled. 
1529=374.	My troops are bound to me as a cow is bound to its calf; 
1533=375.	but like a son who, 
1537=376.	hating his mother, 
1541=377.	leaves his city, 
1545=378.	my princely sister holy @wDN{Inana} has run away from me back to brick-built @wSN{Kulaba}. 
1549=379.	If she loves her city and hates me, 
1553=380.	why does she bind the city to me? 
1557=381.	If she hates the city and yet loves me, 
1561=382.	why does she bind me to the city? 
1565=383.	If the mistress removes herself from me to her holy chamber and abandons me like an Anzud chick, 
1569=384.	then may she at least bring me home to brick-built @wSN{Kulaba}: 
1573=385.	on that day my spear shall be laid aside. 
1577=386.	On that day she may shatter my shield. 
1581=387.	Speak thus to my princely sister, holy @wDN{Inana}.”}”} 

{388 = 388-398}
1585=388.	Holy @wDN{Inana} uttered this response: @q[Inana,Lugalbanda]{“Now, at the end, 
1589=389.	on the banks, in the water-meadows, of a clear river, 
1593=390.	of a river of clear water, 
1597=391.	of the river which is Inana's gleaming waterskin, 
1601=392.	the @fsux{suḫurmaš} fish eats the honey-herb; the toad eats the mountain acorns; 
1605=393.	and the …… fish, 
1609=394.	which is a god of the @fsux{suḫurmaš} fish, 
1614=395.	plays happily there and darts about. 
1618=396.	With his scaly tail he touches the old reeds in that holy place. 
1622=397.	The tamarisks of the place, as many as there are, 
1626=398.	drink water from that pool.”} 

{399 = 399-408}
1630=399.	@q{“It stands alone, it stands alone! One tamarisk stands alone at the side! 
1634=400.	When @wRN{Enmerkar} son of @wDN{Utu} has cut that tamarisk and has fashioned it into a bucket, 
1638=401.	he must tear up the old reeds in that holy place roots and all, 
1642=402.	and collect them in his hands. 
